Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Fix Google sign-in landing on a dead grey screen

An OAuth sign-in never produced a session, and the browser was left on a
screen of loading skeletons that never resolved.

socialAuth asks Supabase to return users to `${origin}/#auth-callback`.
GoTrue does not merge that fragment - on the implicit flow it builds its
own by concatenation (`redirectURL + "#" + params`) - so the browser
actually lands on:

    https://dates.care/#auth-callback#access_token=eyJ...&expires_in=...

Everything after the first # is the fragment, so the fragment is
`auth-callback#access_token=...`. Parsing that with URLSearchParams -
which is exactly what @supabase/auth-js does - produces a key called
`auth-callback#access_token` and no key called `access_token`:

    keys: auth-callback#access_token, expires_in, refresh_token, token_type
    access_token found: false

So detectSessionInUrl found no tokens and no session was ever created.

The same fragment then defeated the router. App compared the whole
fragment against screen names, so `auth-callback#access_token=...`
matched no case and fell through to `default: <Discovery/>` - which,
with nobody signed in, renders skeletons for ever. That grey screen was
the visible half of the bug.

lib/authUrl flattens the double fragment back to the shape auth-js
expects and remembers which screen the redirect was aiming at. It runs
from lib/supabase before createClient, because detectSessionInUrl reads
window.location during client initialisation and only gets one look.
The router now parses the fragment instead of comparing it whole, and
the first render is seeded from the remembered screen so there is no
flash of the wrong page - and no reliance on a fragment auth-js clears
asynchronously once it has consumed the tokens.

Verified by building both revisions and loading the real callback URL in
headless Chromium: before, the skeleton screen; after, the branded
callback screen.

